#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163004} :activity/id #uuid "c9745592-4ec7-557a-9119-d1dcdcf23d32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049149 :topic/id #uuid "de945224-de03-5292-84ca-39d9ddf3f7e4" :topic/math-academy-id 3003} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163288 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the multiplication law for conditional probability for two events ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/5d/5d8b650b90c85fbc0db5e4b6fae61767e293a843d18a2526f3ee4157733e1f4b.svg) ![](images/44/44229a2a35108e36c1348009a552be50e798af9b2807f49c4e4ef8515ed7eefe.svg)\n\nNow suppose that ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) are discrete random variables. The conditional probability that ![](images/2c/2cc57b1c8b00862c078193ea1774f4012e1513ab1a26a6d5bb806e046220a812.svg) *given that* ![](images/46/46851627a248368806191a89ed2bb59d72b83b2e352f768da26f41305e156052.svg) follows immediately from the multiplication law and is given by\n\n![](images/2b/2b705d0acedbb080625148287647d075dce75f40531615a52352a848a85892fe.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) is the joint PMF of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/07/07fd689b67975f50da1b6b025460996fc87f21f758f5e98d34eb84c9b0e50fcd.svg) and ![](images/3a/3aa9c47fc054317f2163393853c05d71714e0c9f0424297f51b46b107e222229.svg) is the marginal mass function of ![](images/02/029615e92d8b6d5c444a4daf74a9a8d63048e8d4be5f97c8207fe7af2e189e18.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- To avoid getting a zero in the denominator, this formula assumes that ![](images/f4/f40e8e149f4e148b5042516dae366a5d17bdc00e1d6e39e27ddf30ece6801d6f.svg)\n- If ![](images/0f/0fa3deb4b6345d48bdeeb06c02ecd3d9bcf6a68cf517412b251a2bcf1e67d07f.svg) then ![](images/ad/ad17cd2272c82a9380977e2ef9ff99fb613681da7df20c06949db320c701bc4c.svg) is not defined by the usual conditional probability formula, since the denominator is zero. In this discrete setting, we therefore restrict the formula to values of ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) for which ![](images/1e/1ebc42b48118a4c62d7203d91e76ecc1a865f8d536cf145c82499ac8fd531cc7.svg)\n\nIn a similar manner, the conditional probability that ![](images/46/46851627a248368806191a89ed2bb59d72b83b2e352f768da26f41305e156052.svg) given that ![](images/2c/2cc57b1c8b00862c078193ea1774f4012e1513ab1a26a6d5bb806e046220a812.svg) can be calculated as ![](images/8c/8c8bdddcd02a672d59ded44b5281cf2f24236f88b2f494730bac998a760e9b0a.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/fd/fdb49440ceb7e11cd9ba0b0869a1e6d3565549969180830d1796db7dd00a2b96.svg) is the marginal mass function of ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) This formula assumes that ![](images/f8/f8020df80e303daf760cd9bf4d8437cc08bc4b95b0ccf64bc7a43ccddc650f5f.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e69256ce-d99e-579e-a7f6-fbd459a410eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5616 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7df83356-4053-5631-85dc-83521ae0811d" :step/math-academy-id 34578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162076 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161876 :question/id #uuid "ddfe8dda-9ab2-5b11-af30-159a7bd32d64" :question/math-academy-id "e-9256"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87675618-337f-5af2-8ad7-9132c080785a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050108}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363273}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "b56cb5f1-be37-5c19-a08d-76523e8ff1db" :step/math-academy-id 34630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163012}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162077 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161877 :question/id #uuid "531b9a19-e424-59e5-8c02-7bca4f67e110" :question/math-academy-id "e-9258"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348dfcb8-2325-5929-b622-5423b7e37a53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410514}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "56d2a08f-a2e0-5602-b510-59f2481df9af" :step/math-academy-id 34637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162104 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161670 :question/id #uuid "2afabb9d-bd38-50f1-9afd-710ab839163c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10049"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410513}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"} :step/id #uuid "000582c8-50ec-5bfc-b34a-c1cec0a03e2c" :step/math-academy-id 37921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162105 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161671 :question/id #uuid "0e42cf0f-f1bd-5762-a4fc-3cedec697c14" :question/math-academy-id "e-10050"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "277b8807-fb74-5ad3-a89b-92dbf90bc075" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410515}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Conditional Probability Mass Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "f16763f3-a2e5-5d38-9f53-ce65ea110dc5" :step/math-academy-id 37922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163325 :tutorial/content "We can now define two more probability mass functions as follows:\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$,** denoted $f_{X|Y}(x|y),$ is defined as $$f_{X|Y}(x|y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y = y) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, \\, Y = y)}{P(Y=y)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)},$$ where $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal mass function of $Y.$\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x,$** denoted $f_{Y|X}(y|x),$ is defined as $$f_{Y|X}(y|x) = P(Y = y \\,|\\, X = x) = \\dfrac{P(X = x,\\, Y = y)}{P(X=x)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)},$$ where $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal mass function of $X.$\n\n**Watch out!** The conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does *not* equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is, in general,\n\n$$f_{X|Y}(x|y) \\ne f_{Y|X}(y|x).$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$f_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_Y(y).$$\n\nIn other words, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then knowing the value of one random variable does not affect the probability distribution of the other." :tutorial/id #uuid "1cbdc2c5-105c-5f9a-b4f4-7e4651bdc789" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6273 :tutorial/title "Conditional Probability Mass Functions"} :step/id #uuid "acc71c94-7794-57f0-bfff-16c3d8ed9e31" :step/math-academy-id 38019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163066}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162164}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410519} :activity/id #uuid "4f58fa0a-3eab-578c-8ede-b29945f6d461" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049149 :topic/id #uuid "de945224-de03-5292-84ca-39d9ddf3f7e4" :topic/math-academy-id 3003} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36eae30e-fa0d-5135-a62d-0465c3393122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410485} {:answer/id #uuid "500b7039-9773-5a4d-a57b-2ec78d1c6cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410489} {:answer/id #uuid "568228f9-502e-5052-aae6-cf24f3f3e87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410490} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2cbc96-2672-5c50-abb6-ff77349a1002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410495} {:answer/id #uuid "b71aacf7-31c6-5c18-bf8d-c7c655c001fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410489} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9630d02-fcc6-50aa-af4a-264fb35a6574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b1e5d993-4954-596b-9a63-c575f6c7babb" :question/math-academy-id "q-142903" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{32}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{32}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{5}{32}$ | $\\frac{5}{32}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=3)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,1)+f(3,2)+f(3,3) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}+\\frac{5}{32}+\\frac{3}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(3,3)=\\frac{3}{32}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{13}{32}$ and $f(3,3)=\\frac{3}{32}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3) & =P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{32})}{(\\frac{13}{32})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "21b7e9f5-9b6f-59bb-8de5-73acd87de7ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c15b04c-20e5-50a7-9307-de1f9df589c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410481} {:answer/id #uuid "309c4fa5-1b46-51a0-8421-56fe01cf985c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410484} {:answer/id #uuid "57efad21-b751-5436-bc49-9e31dc13e0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410491} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfd22a8-559a-59b7-b153-5dfd12c90df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410493} {:answer/id #uuid "74305b04-7264-5ad2-accf-85820a02cffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410491} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2be8906-c901-5824-8831-8ec5b219586c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "51c80586-803f-589a-a591-22c79fbdc1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-142943" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=3)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=y)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,y)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {1,2,3},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,1)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |"} :step/id #uuid "30942c86-dbc3-5faa-928f-8a3e27c6ef2a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dea9c6c-8a37-5b15-a053-45fd4f8d2f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410483} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b9f3ed-06e4-59ef-8471-d8398ce14731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410497} {:answer/id #uuid "d82990b6-7322-5310-a01d-936be990fc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410501} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c7c149-9c87-52d8-aaf5-4888360f519e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410504} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a8127b-532b-5c4e-95ea-e82e38e362d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410501} :answer-field/id #uuid "395c93a7-3493-526c-8001-2fe1cf196ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b172bfa5-07a0-5df8-8d2d-99d558ae1db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142896" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.25$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=9$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3) & =\\frac{P((0<X\\le 6),Y=3)}{P(Y=3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=3,Y=3)+P(X=6,Y=3)}{P(Y=3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,3)+f(6,3)}{{f}_{Y}(3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(3) & =f(0,3)+f(3,3)+f(6,3)+f(9,3) \\\\  & =0+0.1+0+0.1 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,3)=0.1$ and $f(6,3)=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3) & =\\frac{f(3,3)+f(6,3)}{{f}_{Y}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1+0}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0812b16-60e1-5ebb-80af-c271cd3f189c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4176ad50-617b-5bbe-9749-530c4518c952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410486} {:answer/id #uuid "461964e4-b6e1-5a7d-85c6-1b999a2b0407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410487} {:answer/id #uuid "4deb08c4-9a80-57d4-8a91-17d29d971092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410488} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1bcdc1-72d1-5f68-bb9d-cd8c02cd1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410498} {:answer/id #uuid "d1106665-3f25-50f6-bf70-205dfb7d3993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410498} :answer-field/id #uuid "38673cd4-d233-5042-adf0-a6d70114ec93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70462da6-df40-572c-b721-518f7d0fb229" :question/math-academy-id "q-142893" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1)=P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=0)}{P(X=1)}=\\frac{f(1,0)}{{f}_{X}(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(1) & =f(1,0)+f(1,1)+f(1,2) \\\\  & =0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(1,0)=0.25.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(1)=0.6$ and $f(1,0)=0.25$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1) & =P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1,0)}{{f}_{X}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.25}{0.6} \\\\  & ≈0.42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dacbdc7c-ec48-5db6-8025-bca188ffb47d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edd7b5b-c6e3-57a4-8be5-217568bb3909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410482} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0c9436-13dd-5557-a695-bb3800789fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410492} {:answer/id #uuid "d6519b1a-e3ae-5f03-a15a-752660368763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410500} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5e3e81-5bff-5014-b439-496be93167e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410502} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb9920f-ffe3-53da-b1aa-afd4167bb93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410502} :answer-field/id #uuid "970267c4-5873-5e3f-8e48-19af831de5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9f027a74-2cf2-5941-ad79-db682e13a988" :question/math-academy-id "q-142945" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=x,Y=4)}{P(Y=4)}=\\frac{f(x,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in {1,2,3},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.05}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(X=3\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(3,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.05}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |"} :step/id #uuid "e7c09165-5bb0-5525-a272-3f6aee4bdcee"}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410480}]}
